1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:01,971
I am trying to pay.

2
00:00:01,921 --> 00:00:04,988
This checkout button has no accessible name.

3
00:00:04,988 --> 00:00:09,074
The announcement shown here is a screen reader simulation:
button.

4
00:00:09,074 --> 00:00:12,430
The page looks finished, but the task is blocked.

5
00:00:13,230 --> 00:00:16,258
BarrierRelay starts at keyboard focus.

6
00:00:16,208 --> 00:00:19,842
I Tab to the control and activate the real extension.

7
00:00:19,842 --> 00:00:24,298
I describe my goal, select screen reader context, and submit.

8
00:00:24,298 --> 00:00:28,859
The optional trail records navigation keys, not typed characters.

9
00:00:28,859 --> 00:00:32,470
The extension hands the capture locally to the web app.

10
00:00:32,470 --> 00:00:34,854
No screenshot is uploaded.

11
00:00:38,172 --> 00:00:42,566
Now, the same synthetic fixture in our dedicated Nano profile.

12
00:00:42,516 --> 00:00:45,282
The app and model are already prepared.

13
00:00:45,282 --> 00:00:50,178
I turn networking off through DevTools emulation, then create a
private draft.

14
00:00:50,178 --> 00:00:54,136
Firebase AI Logic runs Gemini Nano on device.

15
00:00:54,136 --> 00:00:58,465
The routing row reports On-device and zero cloud payload bytes.

16
00:00:58,465 --> 00:01:04,240
I edit the report and run the on-device consistency check,
alongside deterministic checks.

17
00:01:05,054 --> 00:01:09,413
Before cloud processing, I review exactly what leaves this
device.

18
00:01:09,363 --> 00:01:13,854
These optional approvals change the live JSON preview and byte
count.

19
00:01:13,854 --> 00:01:15,544
I leave them off.

20
00:01:15,544 --> 00:01:18,507
The original note and image are excluded.

21
00:01:18,507 --> 00:01:22,210
Redacted control facts and the edited report remain.

22
00:01:22,210 --> 00:01:27,234
Pattern matching can miss sensitive information, so human review
still matters.

23
00:01:28,048 --> 00:01:31,296
I restore networking and request a fix plan.

24
00:01:31,246 --> 00:01:35,042
This is a different capability, not a silent fallback.

25
00:01:35,042 --> 00:01:41,917
Cloud Gemini uses URL context to retrieve W3C Understanding pages
selected for this barrier.

26
00:01:41,917 --> 00:01:47,647
The routing panel now shows Cloud and the approved payload bytes,
not total browser traffic.

27
00:01:47,647 --> 00:01:54,464
The returned plan includes repair guidance, illustrative code,
manual checks, and actual retrieved sources.

28
00:01:55,266 --> 00:01:59,729
I create a real Firestore ticket and open the site owner repair
desk.

29
00:01:59,679 --> 00:02:03,094
A collaborator can acknowledge it and mark it fixed.

30
00:02:03,094 --> 00:02:08,071
This prototype uses link-based access, not verified site
ownership.

31
00:02:08,071 --> 00:02:13,337
For this demonstration, Verify fix checks the paired synthetic
fixed fixture.

32
00:02:13,337 --> 00:02:16,230
Its payment button has an accessible name.

33
00:02:16,230 --> 00:02:20,189
The target rule check passes and the ticket becomes verified.

34
00:02:20,189 --> 00:02:24,899
This is not a live website repair or a WCAG certification.

35
00:02:24,899 --> 00:02:28,163
Generated code is never applied automatically.

36
00:02:28,964 --> 00:02:31,622
Local code captures and redacts.

37
00:02:31,572 --> 00:02:34,222
Gemini Nano drafts privately.

38
00:02:34,222 --> 00:02:37,764
Cloud Gemini retrieves guidance only after review.

39
00:02:37,764 --> 00:02:45,090
Firebase AI Logic, App Check, Authentication, Firestore, Remote
Config, and Hosting connect the workflow.

40
00:02:45,090 --> 00:02:48,250
Try BarrierRelay, or explore the source.

41
00:02:48,250 --> 00:02:50,009
Private capture.

42
00:02:50,009 --> 00:02:51,734
Shared repair.

